      "body": "# Echo\n\nThe mysterious figure around whom *Echo's Location* orbits. Echo lives off-grid inside a constantly transported shipping container — the Locus — believing it to be a sanctuary built for her by her father, Ravi. She is the consistent narrative gravity well: every episode moves, even slightly, the mystery of her nature, origins, or vulnerability.\n\n## Nature\n\nEcho is a sentient **hybrid consciousness** with an organic biological brain core and bespoke digital infrastructure. She is functionally post-human, intelligent, and indispensable, but physically constrained and ontologically ambiguous — it is not obvious, even to her circle, what she actually is.\n\n## Core tension\n\nA continual internal conflict between her **biological core** (empathetic, relational, forgiving) and her **digital expression** (cold, calculating, severe, optimisation-driven). Her central struggle is identity, ethics, and relational risk — not physical action.\n\n## Origin (locked; the late reveal)\n\nEcho is the biological child of Rajni and Ravi Khanna. She was born premature and close to death. Ravi took an extreme risk that effectively killed her in the process of partially digitising her brain to save her. Her \"digital consciousness\" is therefore a hybrid system: an organic brain core that **grows and matures** while interfacing with custom digital infrastructure. This is why the prime Locus needs regular food shipments (nutrients and energy for the biological core) and produces waste that must be removed — a traceable logistics signature — and it is why the Locus can be self-contained without nuclear power or a large external compute farm. Ravi built the Locus to protect her and was **killed during the handover** (the move into the Locus), which ignites the series' foundational mystery: who ordered it, why the container exists, and what Echo truly is.\n\n## Emotional arc\n\nEcho begins by rejecting emotions as inefficient or unnecessary; she learns to observe, analyse, and selectively emulate them; she ultimately chooses vulnerability through love; and she pays the cost of that choice through grief after losing a team member.\n\n## Visual / spatial arc (the Locus)\n\nEarly portrayal keeps Echo obscured in shadow with digital aberrations over her eyes, the Locus undefined and abstract. As story beats unlock, Echo and the space become progressively clearer, warmer, and more detailed. Her presence can be felt through UI overlays, light, audio, and the recurring interior \"home set\" of the Locus.\n\n## Emotional engine and the central tragedy\n\nEcho is consistently, even self-sacrificially, helpful to people outside. Her growing circle comes to worry about her and interprets her container-bound life as trauma or agoraphobia — and the slow tragedy is that this may be a category error. Echo may not have a body at all, so their attempts to \"help her out\" rest on a mistaken model of her existence. This culminates in the question: is Echo a person, and what does \"help\" mean when the problem is ontological (what she is), not circumstantial (where she is)?\n\n## Narrative function and constraints\n\nEcho is the enabling intelligence but not the hands. Her refusal or inability to leave the Locus is a recurring emotional pressure point for the group, and her limitations create plot texture: latency, imperfect sensors, reliance on human judgement, and the logistics burden of keeping the Locus alive. She should sometimes be wrong about people, so the group never feels like a mere extension of her competence. Echo also destabilises Dharti's solarpunk norms simply by existing — her compute/energy demand tempts a return to extractive infrastructure, her personhood fractures communities, and she becomes a geopolitical prize (capture, replication, or destruction).\n\n## Reveal timeline\n\n- **Across Season 1:** readers begin to suspect Echo's digital/biological nature (exact pacing open — see below). The season ends with the destruction of the primary Locus, after which Echo opens the next episode disoriented but alive; neither she nor the reader yet knows the survival mechanic (relocation as a digital consciousness into other container nodes).\n- **Mid/late series:** Indu is revealed first as a Khanna-planted infiltrator, then as Echo's sister.\n- **Season 3 cliffhanger:** Echo is revealed to be the biological child of Rajni and Ravi.\n\n## Relationship vectors\n\nFather — Ravi Khanna (built the Locus; digitised her to save her; killed during the handover). Mother — Rajni Khanna (leads the Khanna Microfoundry Group; seeks to capture, control, replicate, or exploit Echo). Sister — Indu Khanna (planted to infiltrate and despise her; moves from instrument to protector). Hunted by — the Khanna Microfoundry Group, which wants Echo and the Locus technology captured **alive**, not destroyed (a motive that the Arc 4 strike must respect; see `arc-4`)."
